{{Infobox Japanese clan|home province=[[Yamato Province]]|founder={{ill|Ōtataneko|ja|大田田根子命}}(legendary)<br/>Historically unknown|cadet branches=|surname nihongo=大神|surname=Miwa|image=三本杉紋.jpg|parent house=[[Kamo clan]]}}The '''{{nihongo|Miwa clan|大神氏|Miwa-uji}}''' is a Japanese ''[[Shake (social class)|shake]]'' and [[samurai]] family who have served the [[Ōmiwa Shrine|Miwa Shrine]] for generations.<ref>{{Cite web |title=大神氏:大神氏是日本少有的與古代神明沾邊的姓氏之一，是大神神社的姓氏（為 -百科知識中文網 |url=https://www.jendow.com.tw/wiki/%E5%A4%A7%E7%A5%9E%E6%B0%8F |access-date=2023-02-06 |website=www.jendow.com.tw}}</ref><ref name="wwwharimayacom-2023a" /> The ancestor of the Miwa family is said to be [[Ōkuninushi|Okuninushi no Mikoto]],<ref name="wwwharimayacom-2023a">{{Cite web |title=社家の姓氏-大神氏- |url=http://www.harimaya.com/o_kamon1/syake/kinki/s_omiwa.html |access-date=2023-01-26 |website=www.harimaya.com}}</ref> via {{ill|Ōtataneko|ja|大田田根子命}}.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Suzuki |first=Masanobu |date=2016-05-12 |title=Clans and Religion in Ancient Japan |chapter=1.4 |doi=10.4324/9781315617909|isbn=9781315617909 }}</ref> The Omiwa clan, also known as the '''Ogami clan''', were a prominent family in ancient Japan known for their responsibility in performing state rituals for the [[Yamato Kingship|Yamato Court]]. The clan rose to prominence in the period between the latter half of the 5th century and the 6th century, before the establishment of capital cities such as [[Heijō-kyō|Heijo-kyo]] and [[Heian-kyō|Heian-kyo]]. The clan's main center of influence was the area around [[Mount Miwa|Mt. Miwa]], which was considered a significant religious and political site. According to tradition, the ancestors of the Ogami clan were able to calm the first epidemic in Japan and this belief has been passed down through the generations to the present-day [[Ōmiwa Shrine]]. Recent research has shed light on the role and significance of the Ogami clan in ancient royal power and rituals.<ref>{{Cite web |title=ちくま新書 古代豪族 大神氏（おおみわし）―ヤマト王権と三輪山祭祀 |url=https://www.kinokuniya.co.jp/f/dsg-01-9784480075352 |access-date=2023-01-26 |website=紀伊國屋書店ウェブストア｜オンライン書店｜本、雑誌の通販、電子書籍ストア |language=ja}}</ref>

== History ==
During an epidemic in Japan [[Emperor Sujin]] was given guidance in the form of a dream to seek out a man named {{Nihongo|{{ill|Ōtataneko|ja|大田田根子命}}|太田田根子}} and appoint him as head priest.<ref name="aston150–1642">{{cite book |author=Aston, William George. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=_oEfAAAAYAAJ |title=Nihongi: Chronicles of Japan from the Earliest Times to A.D. 697, Volume 2 |date=1896 |publisher=The Japan Society London |isbn=9780524053478 |pages=150–164 |author-link=William George Aston}}</ref> He eventually found him in [[Izumo Province]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Ellwood |first=Robert S. |date=1990 |title=The Sujin Religious Revolution |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/30234018 |journal=Japanese Journal of Religious Studies |volume=17 |issue=2/3 |pages=199–217 |doi=10.18874/jjrs.17.2-3.1990.199-217 |jstor=30234018 |issn=0304-1042|doi-access=free }}</ref> When he was found and installed, the pestilence eventually subsided, allowing five cereal crops to ripen.<ref name="aston150–1642" /> Out of an abundance of caution, the Emperor also appointed {{Nihongo|Ikagashikoo|伊香色雄}} as {{Nihongo|''kami-no-mono-akatsu-hito''|神班物者}}, or one who sorts the offerings to the gods.<ref name="chamberlain-basil-1882a">{{cite book |author=Chamberlain, Basil |url=http://www.sacred-texts.com/shi/kj/kj072.htm |title=''[SECT. LXV.—EMPEROR SŪ-JIN (PART III: STORY OF OHO-TATA-NE-KO'S BIRTH)]'' (''The Kojiki'') |publisher=Read before the Asiatic Society of Japan on April 12, May 10, and June 21, 1882, reprinted in 1919 |page=219 |quote=His Augustness Oho-tata-ne-ko&nbsp;... was the ancestor of the Dukes of Miwa and of the Dukes of Kamo. |author-link=Basil Chamberlain}}</ref> To this day the [[Miwa clan|Miwa sept]] of the [[Kamo clan]] claim to be descents from {{ill|Ōtataneko|ja|大田田根子命}}, while Ikagashikoo was a claimed ancestor of the now extinct [[Mononobe clan]].<ref name="chamberlain-basil-1882a" />

In the 8th year of the reign of [[Emperor Tenmu|Emperor Temmu]], the Miwa clan was renamed Miwa-no-kun and given the surname Ogami-chosin.{{clarify|date=March 2023}} The descendants of Koichi Maro's son Shinobito{{clarify|date=March 2023}} served as the Ogami High Priestess. According to notes in the genealogy, the Daiminushi family{{clarify|date=March 2023}} served the [[Southern Court]] during the period of the [[Nanboku-chō period|Nanboku-cho period]]. Many members of the family died in the civil wars between the Northern and Southern dynasties.<ref name="wwwharimayacom-2023a" />

After the [[Muromachi period]], the legitimate lineage served as high priests, but some of the family members became warriors and served the Miyoshi and [[Kitabatake clan|Kitabatake clans]]. After the Hobo break,{{clarify|date=March 2023}} the Daikami family took the name Takamiya and served the Ogami Shrine, and their descendants continued to serve the shrine until the [[Meiji Restoration]].<ref name="wwwharimayacom-2023a" />

One branch of the Miwa family, known as the Hiyoshi, served the [[Usa Jingū]] in ancient times. Their descendants served as the chief priest of the Usa Jingu for a long time until they were replaced by the Usa clan. From their descendants, medieval warriors such as Ogata, Ono, and Anan were born,{{clarify|date=March 2023}} and during the [[Sengoku period]], they played a part in the military power of the [[Ōtomo clan|Otomo clan]], the feudal lords in [[Kyushu]].<ref name="wwwharimayacom-2023a" />

